1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,488
This is MATH 239, video 23 b. The topics are two applications of breadth-first search, and minimum spanning trees. We follow sections 5.5 and 5.6 of the graph theory notes.

2
00:00:15,938 --> 00:00:29,805
After this video, you can use breadth-first search to find an odd cycle or a bipartition. You can use it to find distances. You can run Prim's algorithm, and prove that it gives a minimum spanning tree.

3
00:00:30,255 --> 00:00:54,127
Theorem 5.5.1 turns the bipartite test into an algorithm. Take a connected graph G and a breadth-first search tree T. G has an odd cycle if and only if some non-tree edge joins two vertices at the same level. Here is the B F S tree of Figure 5.4 again, with the four non-tree edges dashed.

4
00:00:54,577 --> 00:01:07,419
The edge 3 10 joins two vertices at level 1. The tree path from 3 to 10 goes up to 9 and down again. With the edge 3 10, it closes a cycle of length 3.

5
00:01:07,869 --> 00:01:25,320
The edge 5 6 joins two vertices at level 2. The tree path 5, 8, 6 and the edge close another 3-cycle. So this graph is not bipartite. The other two non-tree edges join adjacent levels and give no odd cycle.

6
00:01:25,770 --> 00:01:39,615
Now the proof. Part i: a same-level non-tree edge gives an odd cycle. We use the B F S tree of problem set 5.4, number 3, from video 23 a. The root is 1.

7
00:01:40,065 --> 00:01:48,300
Take a non-tree edge u v with both ends at the same level. Here u is 3 and v is 7, both at level 3.

8
00:01:48,750 --> 00:02:07,374
Follow the parent arrows up from u and from v. The two paths first meet at some vertex, m levels higher. Since u and v are different, m is at least 1. Here they meet at vertex 2, at level 1. So m equals 2. The meeting vertex can also be the root.

9
00:02:07,824 --> 00:02:18,960
The tree path from u to v goes up m levels and down m levels. So it has 2 m edges. Here the path is 3, 4, 2, 6, 7, with 4 edges.

10
00:02:19,410 --> 00:02:32,743
Add the edge u v. The result is a cycle of length 2 m plus 1, which is odd. Here it is a 5-cycle. The edge 7 9 at level 3 gives a 7-cycle in the same way.

11
00:02:33,193 --> 00:02:47,103
Part ii: if no non-tree edge joins equal levels, G is bipartite. Here is breadth-first search on the 3-cube from 0 0 0. The level of each vertex is its number of ones.

12
00:02:47,553 --> 00:03:11,233
There is no same-level edge. Tree edges join levels that differ by exactly 1. By the primary property, every other edge joins levels that differ by at most 1, and not 0, so also by 1. So each edge joins an odd level to an even level. Put the odd levels in A and the even levels in B. This is a bipartition.

13
00:03:11,683 --> 00:03:28,195
So G is bipartite, and by Theorem 5.3.2 it has no odd cycle. Together, parts i and ii prove the theorem. The proof is constructive: B F S gives either an odd cycle or a bipartition.

14
00:03:28,645 --> 00:03:47,930
We do not need the whole tree first. The notes check during the search. Each time you look at the vertex at the head of the queue, check whether it has an edge to a tree vertex at the same level. Here is B F S from vertex 1 again. After 1 is exhausted, the head is 2, at level 1.

15
00:03:48,380 --> 00:04:03,121
Vertex 14 is already in the tree, also at level 1, and 2 14 is an edge. So the paths up to the root give the odd cycle 2, 1, 14, 2. We can stop: the graph is not bipartite.

16
00:04:03,571 --> 00:04:25,929
The second application is shortest paths. Theorem 5.5.2: in a connected graph, the length of a shortest path from u to v equals the level of v in any breadth-first search tree with root u. If v is at level k, the tree path from v up to u has k edges. So the distance is at most k.

17
00:04:26,379 --> 00:04:44,149
Now take any path from u to v in G. It starts at level 0 and ends at level k. By the primary property, no edge joins vertices more than one level apart. So each edge moves at most one level, and the path has at least k edges.

18
00:04:44,599 --> 00:04:53,346
So the distance from u to v equals the level of v. The length of a shortest path is called the distance between the vertices.

19
00:04:53,796 --> 00:05:10,137
Here is the graph of problem set 5.4, number 3. The purple numbers are the distances from vertex 1. They are the levels of the B F S tree from video 23 a. For example, vertex 8 is at distance 4.

20
00:05:10,587 --> 00:05:34,417
Problem set 5.5, number 3 asks for an algorithm for the diameter. The diameter is the largest distance between two vertices. One algorithm: run breadth-first search from every vertex. Each search gives all distances from its root. The largest level over all searches is the diameter. It works by Theorem 5.5.2.

21
00:05:34,867 --> 00:05:44,808
For this graph, the largest level is 3 or 4 for each root. So the diameter is 4. For example, the distance from 1 to 8 is 4.

22
00:05:45,258 --> 00:06:01,151
An exercise from the notes. Pause the video and try it. A connected graph has m vertices at distance n from a vertex v, with n at least 1. Prove that some spanning tree has at least m vertices of degree 1.

23
00:06:01,601 --> 00:06:21,591
Take a breadth-first search tree with root v. By Theorem 5.5.2, distance equals level. So the m vertices are exactly the vertices at level n. In the example, v is 1 and n is 3. The orange vertices 3, 7 and 9 are at level 3.

24
00:06:22,041 --> 00:06:42,393
Start at one of these vertices and go down to a child, again and again, until a vertex has no child. This vertex is a leaf. Its level is at least n, which is at least 1, so it is not the root. A non-root leaf has degree 1. From 3 we reach 8. 7 and 9 are leaves already.

25
00:06:42,843 --> 00:06:55,536
Vertices below different level-n vertices are different, because each vertex has only one parent. So the m leaves are different, and T has at least m vertices of degree 1.

26
00:06:55,986 --> 00:07:14,781
Another exercise. Pause the video and try it. Describe a procedure to find a maximal bipartite subgraph of a connected graph. Maximal means no edge of G can be added back without losing the property. The notes do not ask for a proof, but we give the reason.

27
00:07:15,231 --> 00:07:26,943
Build a breadth-first search tree. Then delete every non-tree edge that joins two vertices at the same level. In this graph, delete 3 10 and 5 6, in red.

28
00:07:27,393 --> 00:07:46,145
Every remaining edge joins levels one apart, so odd levels and even levels give a bipartition. If we put back a deleted edge, the proof of Theorem 5.5.1 gives an odd cycle. So no deleted edge can come back, and the subgraph is maximal.

29
00:07:46,595 --> 00:08:03,512
Section 5.6 is about the minimum spanning tree problem, or M S T. We have a connected graph G, and a weight on each edge. The weight of a subgraph is the sum of the weights of its edges. We want a spanning tree with the smallest weight.

30
00:08:03,962 --> 00:08:18,767
Here is the example of the notes. The blue spanning tree uses the edges a c, c e, c d, d b and d f. Its weight is 4 plus 8 plus 8 plus 8 plus 3, which is 31.

31
00:08:19,217 --> 00:08:31,271
The notes ask: is there a spanning tree of smaller weight? The edge weights could be costs to build links in a network. A minimum spanning tree connects every location at the least cost.

32
00:08:31,721 --> 00:08:48,958
We need one tool from video 22, Theorem 5.2.3. If T is a spanning tree and e is an edge not in T, then T plus e has exactly one cycle. If we remove any edge of that cycle, we get a spanning tree again.

33
00:08:49,408 --> 00:08:54,720
Use it on the tree of weight 31. Add the edge a e, with weight 5.

34
00:08:55,170 --> 00:09:02,317
T plus a e has one cycle: a, c, e, and back to a. It uses a c, c e and the new edge.

35
00:09:02,767 --> 00:09:18,105
Remove c e, which has weight 8. The result is a spanning tree of weight 31 plus 5 minus 8, which is 28. So 31 was not the minimum. Prim's algorithm avoids this trial and error.

36
00:09:18,555 --> 00:09:41,361
Prim's algorithm grows the tree one edge at a time, like Algorithm 5.4.1. Start with any vertex. While T is not spanning, look at the edges in the cut induced by the vertex set of T. These are the edges with one end in T and one end outside. Pick one with the smallest weight, and add it with its new vertex.

37
00:09:41,811 --> 00:09:55,229
Why is there always an edge to pick? T is not spanning, so its vertex set is a nonempty proper subset. G is connected, so by Theorem 4.8.5 the cut is not empty.

38
00:09:55,679 --> 00:10:08,949
Here is Prim's algorithm on the example, starting at a, as in the notes. Orange dashed edges are the cut. The thick orange edge is the one we pick. Green edges are already in the tree.

39
00:10:09,399 --> 00:10:16,268
The cut of a is a b 9, a c 4 and a e 5. The smallest is a c, weight 4. Add c.

40
00:10:16,718 --> 00:10:30,201
Now the cut of a, c is a e 5, c d 8, c e 8 and a b 9. Pick a e, weight 5. The total is 9. Note that c e is no longer a candidate after e joins.

41
00:10:30,651 --> 00:10:37,285
The cut is now e f 7, c d 8 and a b 9. Pick e f. The total is 16.

42
00:10:37,735 --> 00:10:44,925
With f in the tree, d f 3 enters the cut. It is the smallest. Pick it. The total is 19.

43
00:10:45,375 --> 00:10:53,375
Only b is left. The cut is b f 7, b d 8 and a b 9. Pick b f. The total is 26.

44
00:10:53,825 --> 00:11:08,011
The tree is spanning, so the algorithm stops. The weight is 26, which is less than 31 and 28. This graph has 75 spanning trees, and none of them has a weight less than 26.

45
00:11:08,461 --> 00:11:38,093
Why does this greedy choice give a minimum spanning tree? Theorem 5.6.1 says Prim's algorithm produces a minimum spanning tree. Let T 1 up to T n be the trees in the algorithm. T k plus 1 is T k plus the edge e k. We prove by induction that some minimum spanning tree contains T k. For k equals 1, T 1 is one vertex, and every M S T contains it. The blue region is the vertex set of T k.

46
00:11:38,543 --> 00:11:55,013
Induction step. Let T star be an M S T that contains T k. If T star also contains e k, it contains T k plus 1, and we are done. If not, look at T star plus e k. The orange edge is e k.

47
00:11:55,463 --> 00:12:13,532
By Theorem 5.2.3, T star plus e k has exactly one cycle C. Remove e k from C. What is left is a path in T star between the two ends of e k. One end is in T k and the other is not. The green path shows it.

48
00:12:13,982 --> 00:12:31,305
The path starts inside V of T k and ends outside. So at least one of its edges, e prime, crosses the cut. It is red. By Theorem 5.2.3, T prime equals T star plus e k minus e prime is a spanning tree.

49
00:12:31,755 --> 00:12:47,157
Prim's algorithm chose e k as an edge of smallest weight in this cut. e prime is also in the cut, so w of e prime is at least w of e k. So the weight of T prime is at most the weight of T star.

50
00:12:47,607 --> 00:13:07,106
T star is a minimum spanning tree, so w of T prime cannot be smaller. So the weights are equal, and T prime is also a minimum spanning tree. It contains T k plus 1. By induction, some M S T contains T n. T n is spanning, so it is that M S T.

51
00:13:07,556 --> 00:13:18,095
An exercise. Pause the video and try it. Run Prim's algorithm on the same graph, but start at d. Give the edges in order, and the total weight.

52
00:13:18,545 --> 00:13:24,390
The cut of d is d f 3, b d 8 and c d 8. Pick d f, weight 3.

53
00:13:24,840 --> 00:13:33,672
The cut of d, f has b f 7 and e f 7. This is a tie. Either one is allowed. We pick b f. The total is 10.

54
00:13:34,122 --> 00:13:38,794
Now e f 7 is the smallest. Pick it. The total is 17.

55
00:13:39,244 --> 00:13:45,559
With e in the tree, a e 5 enters the cut. It is the smallest. The total is 22.

56
00:13:46,009 --> 00:13:54,457
Last, the cut of the five vertices is a c 4, c d 8 and c e 8. Pick a c. The total is 26.

57
00:13:54,907 --> 00:14:05,296
We get the same five edges and the same weight 26, in a different order. This agrees with the theorem: every start gives a minimum spanning tree.

58
00:14:05,746 --> 00:14:24,029
To recap. In a breadth-first search tree, the graph has an odd cycle exactly when a non-tree edge joins two vertices at the same level. That edge and the tree path give a cycle of length 2 m plus 1. Otherwise the odd and even levels give a bipartition.

59
00:14:24,479 --> 00:14:34,463
The level of a vertex is its distance from the root. So breadth-first search finds shortest paths, and running it from every vertex gives the diameter.

60
00:14:34,913 --> 00:14:52,342
A minimum spanning tree has the least total weight. Prim's algorithm grows a tree and always adds a cheapest edge of the cut. The proof exchanges one edge of an optimal tree, and the weight does not increase. The next video starts planar graphs.
